.TH CHTTPSVR_STOP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_stop \- close this server's listener
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_stop(chttpsvr srv);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Removes the listener socket that chttpsvr_start registered from the
shared reactor and closes it.  For a Unix domain socket listener it
then removes the socket file, but only while its path still names the
socket that this server created.  It returns at once; the shared
reactor and every other registered listener keep running unaffected.

It is idempotent: calling chttpsvr_stop on a server that never
started, or on one that has already stopped, does nothing.

It silently does nothing when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID or a stale
handle that has already been destroyed.

Connections that the server accepted before this call keep running;
the server does not close them.  A request in progress continues until
it completes, and a keep-alive connection continues to be served,
request after request, for as long as the client keeps it open, until
the idle timeout closes it as it would on a running server.  This
holds even across a later restart with chttpsvr_start on the same
handle, which then serves such a connection under its new
configuration.  chttpsvr_destroy waits for such in-flight requests to
drain completely, and closes every remaining idle connection, before
it frees the resources of the server.

It is safe to call this function with CHTTPSVR_INVALID (0).

It can run concurrently with a chttpsvr_start call that restarts the
same handle on a different thread: rather than racing this call, the
restart waits for the teardown of the old listener here to finish
completely and then binds a new one.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
This function is synchronous and NOT async-signal-safe.  Do not call
it directly from a signal handler; use chttpsvr_engine_stop() there
instead.  It takes internal locks that the interrupted thread may
already hold, for example inside chttpsvr_start() or
chttpsvr_destroy(), in which case the calling thread deadlocks itself.
For a signal-driven shutdown hook, use chttpsvr_engine_stop(): only
that function is documented as async-signal-safe, because it moves
all of this work off the interrupted thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
